  "final_message": "## Summary\n\nFixed `humanize.intcomma` in `src/humanize/number.py` so it no longer raises `OverflowError` for Python integers outside the float range.\n\n### Change\nThe function previously relied on float-backed `,` formatting (e.g. `f\"{value:,}\"`), which fails for integers larger than the float range. I added a path that formats arbitrarily large positive and negative integers by grouping their decimal digits directly from the string representation, without any float conversion. The existing behavior for ordinary integers (and the `ndigits`, `thousands_sep`, and `decimal_sep` options) is preserved.\n\n### Verification\n- Configured checks: `PYTHONPATH=src python -m pytest -q tests/test_number.py` — **passed**.\n- Manual reproduction:\n  - `humanize.intcomma(10**400)` → comma-grouped string of correct length (534 chars for the 400-digit number plus separators).\n  - Negative large integers (e.g. `-(10**400)`) are grouped with the leading sign preserved.\n  - Ordinary values remain unchanged: `12345 -> \"12,345\"`, `1,234,567 | 1,234,567.25 | 1,234.55`.\n  - Special floats still behave as before: `1,000 | -Inf | NaN | None`, and string inputs like `'1e5'` / `'+Inf'` still round-trip correctly.\n\n### Remaining limitations\nOnly Python `int` values bypass the float path; `float('inf')`/`float('nan')` continue through the existing formatting logic unchanged.",
